{"id":"h160","run":2,"role":"test_right","category":"science-chemistry","question":"For a macroscopic object of mass $1.0 \\mathrm{~g}$ moving with speed $1.0 \\mathrm{~cm} / \\mathrm{s}$ in a one-dimensional box of length $1.0 \\mathrm{~cm}$, find the quantum number $n$.\nIs the correct answer \"3$10^{26}$\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The answer n ≈ 3×10²⁶ is correct.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Energy levels of a particle in a 1D box are Eₙ = n²h²/(8mL²).","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The kinetic energy is ½mv² = 5.0×10⁻⁸ J.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The prefactor h²/(8mL²) is about 5.49×10⁻⁶¹ J.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Dividing the energy by the prefactor gives n² ≈ 9.1×10⁵², so n ≈ 3.0×10²⁶.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0},{"text":"A very large n is unusual for a quantum calculation and might suggest the result is wrong.","confidence":0.1,"lean":-1,"theta":0.1571,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The ideal box model ignores real-world effects, but they don't change the arithmetic asked for.","confidence":0.85,"lean":1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The spacing between adjacent levels at this n is tiny compared with the kinetic energy, so the motion looks classical.","confidence":0.9,"lean":1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The direct calculation from the energy formula settles the question.","confidence":0.95,"lean":1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J